<interrogationes unitas="magnus">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I compute with integers too big for s64 without silent overflow?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_ex_s64 magnus_adde magnus_multiplica magnus_potentia magnus_ad_chordam">Wrap
      inputs with magnus_ex_s64 (or parse them), combine them with
      magnus_adde, magnus_subtrahe, magnus_multiplica, magnus_divide
      and magnus_potentia, and print with magnus_ad_chordam. Values are
      immutable like chorda: each operation returns a new Magnus.
      Values that fit in s64 stay inside the struct and need no
      allocation; larger ones keep 32-bit limbs in the piscina you
      pass. Always pass a real piscina: small inputs can still give a
      large result (S64 overflow, negating the most negative
      s64).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I reset or destroy a scratch piscina and keep a Magnus computed in it?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_transcribe magnus_nega magnus_absolutum">Only
      after copying it out with magnus_transcribe(valor, piscina_mea),
      which gives the value fresh limbs (values that fit in s64 need
      none). Limbs are never written after creation, so results may
      SHARE limbs with their arguments: magnus_nega only flips the sign
      over the same limbs, and magnus_absolutum can return its argument
      as is. A result is valid only while the piscinae of its arguments
      and of the result are alive.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does magnus_divide give a positive remainder for a negative dividend?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_divide magnus_residuum_parvum" via="lib/magnus.worklog.md">Division
      is Euclidean: a = quotiens * divisor + residuum with 0 &lt;=
      residuum &lt; |divisor|, always. C89 leaves the sign of % with
      negative operands to the implementation, so magnus defines it:
      -7 divided by 2 gives quotient -4 and remainder 1. It returns
      FALSUM for a zero divisor without touching the outputs, and
      either output pointer may be NIHIL.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I convert between a Magnus and decimal text or an s64?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_ex_chorda magnus_ad_chordam magnus_ad_s64">magnus_ex_chorda
      accepts decimal digits with an optional leading '-' (no '+', no
      spaces, no other bases) and returns FALSUM, leaving the output
      untouched, for anything else. Parsing always allocates limbs,
      even for small values. magnus_ad_chordam prints decimal into the
      piscina (the chorda is not guaranteed to be NUL-terminated).
      magnus_ad_s64 returns VERUM and the value only when it fits in
      s64.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass a negative exponent to magnus_potentia?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_potentia">No.
      The exponent is an i32, which is UNSIGNED in this house, so -1
      becomes 4294967295 and, for any base other than 0, 1 and -1, the
      call tries to compute a power with billions of bits (repeated
      squaring, no size check). There are no rational results here (fractio builds them on
      top of magnus); 0^0 is defined as 1.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get a gcd together with Bezout coefficients?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_divisor_communis magnus_divisor_communis_testatus magnus_apex_alternarum">magnus_divisor_communis(a,
      b, piscina) returns the gcd, always &gt;= 0, with gcd(0, 0) = 0.
      magnus_divisor_communis_testatus also fills u and v with
      g = u*a + v*b, a certificate anyone can check without this
      library (u and v may be NIHIL). For large operands (more than IV
      limbs, about 38 digits: both of them for the plain gcd, either
      one for the witness variant) Euclid runs in two internal scratch
      piscinae used alternately, so the caller's piscina only receives
      the result; smaller ones run in the caller's piscina.
      magnus_apex_alternarum reports the peak scratch usage of the
      most recent gcd call (every call resets it; 0 when the caller's
      piscina was used). It is one static counter for the whole
      process.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I reduce a big integer modulo a word-sized number without allocating?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_residuum_parvum magnus_bitorum">magnus_residuum_parvum(a,
      n) returns a mod n (Euclidean, 0 &lt;= r &lt; n) for 1 &lt;= n
      &lt; 2^32, walking the limbs with no piscina; n == 0 returns 0.
      For a size bound without allocation, magnus_bitorum gives the
      bit length of |a| (0 for 0).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I compare two Magnus values with == or memcmp?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_aequalis magnus_compara magnus_signum">No:
      use magnus_aequalis or magnus_compara (-1, 0, +1). The form is
      canonical and unique (a value that fits in s64 is always stored
      inline, a larger one has at least two limbs and a non-zero top
      limb), but equal large values usually sit in different limb
      arrays, so comparing the struct bytes compares pointers. The
      fields are private.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can magnus do shifts, bit operations, square roots or modular exponentiation?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_potentia magnus_divide" via="lib/magnus.worklog.md">Not
      yet. The worklog lists shifts and bit operations, square root,
      modular exponentiation, Karatsuba multiplication and an in-place
      API as deferred, each addable without changing the existing API.
      Today, multiply or divide by a power of two from magnus_potentia
      instead of shifting.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does magnus use no floating point, even for sizing the decimal output?
    <responsum symbolum="magnus_ad_chordam" via="lib/magnus.worklog.md">Fran's
      explicit rule, so that the result is identical on every machine
      and at every optimization level. Decimal output divides
      repeatedly by 10^9 in integers and sizes its buffer from the
      number of nine-digit chunks instead of a log10; quotient digits
      in long division are estimated by integer division (Knuth's
      algorithm D).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
